I’ll inspect the pattern metadata and available diffraction tooling first, then index/search candidate phases, refine the best structural mixture in GSAS-II, and validate that every submitted artifact comes from the final project. I’ll keep phase assignments conservative where the data do not uniquely support them.

The measurement is raw Cu Kα reflection data, with no supplied uncertainties or calibrated profile, and the chemistry is constrained only to the Mn–W–O precursor system after mild 300 °C heating. I’m now extracting peak positions and checking the local phase-search/indexing interfaces before committing to candidate structures.

The dominant peak triplet near 23–25° and the accompanying lines near 29°, 34°, 42°, and 50° strongly resemble a distorted tungsten oxide pattern, while weaker features could contain manganese oxide or reaction product. I’m comparing all locally available Mn–O, W–O, and Mn–W–O structures against the full trace, including a possible systematic 2θ offset and Kα doublet.

Two phases are now supported by independent lines: monoclinic WO₃ accounts for the 23–25° triplet and most W-dominated peaks, while rutile-type β‑MnO₂ uniquely explains peaks at about 37.67°, 43.1°, 56.96°, 59.66°, and 72.6°. MnWO₄ would require strong 30–31° peaks that are absent. I’m moving to a two-phase GSAS-II refinement, with phase fractions derived from refined scale factors and with the instrumental offset/profile treated explicitly.

The first full two-phase refinement already reaches \(R_{wp}\approx6.85\%\) with about 69.7 wt% WO₃ and 30.3 wt% β‑MnO₂. Before finalizing, I’m screening the plausible monoclinic/triclinic WO₃ reference models under the same refinement recipe; this tests whether the phase fraction depends materially on the chosen WO₃ polymorph/setting.

The model screen is decisive: the Loopstra–Boldrini monoclinic γ‑WO₃ model (COD 2311041, \(P2_1/c\), an alternate cell setting) gives \(R_{wp}\approx6.85\%\), versus about 8.07% for another monoclinic coordinate set and ≥15% for orthorhombic/triclinic alternatives. Its fraction remains near 70 wt%, so the phase assignment and abundance are stable. I’m checking the residual for any missing crystalline phase and then tightening only defensible profile/structural parameters.

The residual contains no independent unmatched peak series; its largest features are derivative/intensity errors exactly at modeled WO₃/MnO₂ reflections, so a third crystalline phase is not warranted. A physically appropriate Bragg–Brentano specimen-height correction gives the same fractions and slightly better fit than a constant zero offset; refining the previously unknown effective polarization improves \(R_{wp}\) to about 6.49% while keeping WO₃ near 70.2 wt%. I’m testing a small set of remaining physically meaningful corrections and will reject any unstable or highly correlated additions.

The retained refinement model is now fixed: two phases, specimen-height correction, refined cells/scales/effective polarization, and isotropic crystallite-size plus microstrain terms. Preferred orientation refined to essentially unity, axial divergence was strongly correlated with specimen height, and atomic-coordinate trials were >97% correlated, so those additions are being excluded. This leaves a stable quantitative model with an expected fraction near 71/29 and makes the coordinate limitation explicit rather than over-refining it.

The reproducible final refinement completed cleanly with no SVD singularity and a final maximum shift/e.s.d. below 0.1. I’m now validating the saved project against the exported CIFs and profile point-for-point, then I’ll write the concise uncertainty report and remove only the temporary screening artifacts.

Identified two crystalline phases:

- Monoclinic γ‑WO₃: 71.00 wt%
- β‑MnO₂ (pyrolusite): 29.00 wt%

Fractions are normalized over modeled crystalline material; realistic uncertainty is about ±2 wt%. No crystalline MnWO₄ was resolved. Final fit: \(R_{wp}=6.105\%\), GoF = 2.270.

